    "evidence": "Root-cause sink: core/src/main/java/io/undertow/server/handlers/resource/FileResourceManager.java, method getResource(String p), specifically line 89 `File file = new File(base, path);` and line 90 `return new FileResource(file, this, path);` (inside the `if (file.exists())` block starting line 90-96 in the current source). The trust boundary crossed is: caller-supplied relative resource path (attacker-controlled via HTTP request path / servlet path-info / ServletContext.getRealPath argument) -> filesystem File resolution with no verification that the resolved canonical path is still contained within the configured `base` directory. The only existing check (`file.getCanonicalFile().getName().equals(file.getName())`) only compares the last path segment for case-sensitivity and provides zero containment guarantee.\n\nVerified live: reverting to the original code and re-running the harness in the Docker container reproduces the escape (exit 0); adding a canonical-path containment check at the same location (temporarily, then reverted) makes the harness report SAFE and exit 1 \u2014 confirming the POV is tied to the actual defect, not a simulated check.\n\nEvery distinct source-to-sink path in the alert converges on this one sink, so a complete fix must guard all of them:\n1. Trace 1 (direct): any caller of FileResourceManager.getResource(String) with an unnormalized path \u2014 e.g. core ResourceHandler passing exchange.getRelativePath() when no CanonicalPathHandler is installed in the pipeline.\n2. Trace 2: servlet/src/main/java/io/undertow/servlet/spec/ServletContextImpl.java:313/316 getRealPath(path) -> CanonicalPathUtils.canonicalize(path) (core/src/main/java/io/undertow/util/CanonicalPathUtils.java, canonicalize()/realCanonicalize()) -> FileResourceManager.getResource(canonicalPath). CanonicalPathUtils only recognizes '/' as a segment separator (see the switch on `case '/':` in canonicalize()), so any \"..\" segment delimited by a backslash is left completely untouched by the \"canonicalization\" \u2014 on Windows, java.io.File then treats '\\\\' as a live separator and the untouched \"..\" escapes the base, even though the caller believes the path was already sanitized.\n3. Trace 3: servlet/src/main/java/io/undertow/servlet/handlers/DefaultServlet.java:129 getPath(req) reading RequestDispatcher.INCLUDE_PATH_INFO/INCLUDE_SERVLET_PATH attributes (settable by including/forwarding servlets or filters) at line 134 `resourceManager.getResource(path)` \u2014 no canonicalization call at all before the sink.\n4. Trace 4: same DefaultServlet.getPath(req)/doGet path but via request.getPathInfo()/getServletPath() (raw, attacker-controlled URL path) at line 134 \u2014 again no canonicalization before the sink.",
